    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-false-discovery-rate-and-familywise-error-rate",
        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "False discovery proportion, FDR, and FWER",
        "statement": "Let $m\\ge1$ be a finite number of hypotheses and write\n$[m]=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$. Fix a probability law $P$ and a set\n$I_0\\subseteq[m]$ of indices whose null hypotheses are true under $P$. A\nmeasurable rejection rule is a measurable map\n$\\mathcal R:\\Omega\\to2^{[m]}$, where the finite set $2^{[m]}$ has its discrete\nsigma-algebra. Set\n\n$$R=|\\mathcal R|,\\qquad V=|\\mathcal R\\cap I_0|.$$\n\nThe **false discovery proportion** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{FDP}:=\\frac{V}{R\\vee1},\\qquad R\\vee1:=\\max(R,1),$$\n\nand the **false discovery rate** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{FDR}:=\\mathbb E_P\\!\\left[\\frac{V}{R\\vee1}\\right].$$\n\nThe **family-wise error rate** is\n\n$$\\operatorname{FWER}:=P(V\\ge1).$$\n\nSince $V\\le R$ whenever $R>0$ and $V=0$ when $R=0$, the FDP is a measurable\nrandom variable in $[0,1]$ and its expectation is finite. These definitions\nuse the fixed true-null set for the specified law; they do not assert that FDR\nand FWER are equal except in the global-null case.",
        "deps": [
          "def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable",
          "def-probability-measure"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg, Controlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing, JRSS B 57(1) (1995), §2 and §3.1",
              "url": "https://www.math.tau.ac.il/~yekutiel/eBayes/bh_1995.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "alg-benjamini-hochberg-step-up-procedure",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "Benjamini–Hochberg step-up procedure",
        "statement": "Input $m\\ge1$ p-values $p_1,\\ldots,p_m\\in[0,1]$ and a level $q\\in(0,1)$.\nOrder the hypotheses by the keys $(p_i,i)$, so equal p-values are ordered by\ntheir original index, and write the ordered values as\n$p_{(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le p_{(m)}$. Set\n\n$$R:=\\max\\bigl(\\{k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}:p_{(k)}\\le kq/m\\}\\cup\\{0\\}\\bigr).$$\n\nReject exactly the hypotheses at ranks $1,\\ldots,R$. If the set in the\nmaximum is empty, then $R=0$ and no hypothesis is rejected. This is a\ndeterministic step-up rule for the supplied p-values; its FDR guarantee is a\nseparate theorem.",
        "deps": [],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg, Controlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing, JRSS B 57(1) (1995), §3.1",
              "url": "https://www.math.tau.ac.il/~yekutiel/eBayes/bh_1995.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "STATS 200: Introduction to Statistical Inference, Lecture 11: Testing Multiple Hypotheses, Stanford University, PDF pp. 28–29",
              "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/stats/stats200/stats200.1172/Lecture11.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 0
      },
      {
        "id": "lem-benjamini-hochberg-leave-one-out-identity",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "title": "Leave-one-out identity for the BH step-up rule",
        "statement": "Let $m\\ge1$, $q\\in(0,1)$, and let $P_1,\\ldots,P_m$ be measurable\n$[0,1]$-valued functions on one measurable space. Apply the algorithm in\n[[alg-benjamini-hochberg-step-up-procedure]]. For each $i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$\nand vector $x_{-i}\\in[0,1]^{m-1}$, order the remaining values as\n$x_{-i,(1)}\\le\\cdots\\le x_{-i,(m-1)}$ and define\n\n$$s_i(x_{-i}):=\\max\\bigl(\\{0\\}\\cup\\{k\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m-1\\}:x_{-i,(k)}\\le(k+1)q/m\\}\\bigr).$$\n\nWhen $m=1$, set $s_i=0$. Let $S_i=s_i(P_{-i})$, let $R$ be the number of\nrejections, and let $I_i$ be the indicator that hypothesis $i$ is rejected.\nThen $S_i$ is measurable with respect to the sigma-algebra generated by the\nother coordinates and, pointwise,\n\n$$\\frac{I_i}{R\\vee1}=\\frac{\\mathbf 1\\{P_i\\le(S_i+1)q/m\\}}{S_i+1}.$$",
        "deps": [
          "alg-benjamini-hochberg-step-up-procedure",
          "def-borel-sigma-algebra",
          "def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces"
        ],
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Yoav Benjamini and Yosef Hochberg, Controlling the False Discovery Rate: A Practical and Powerful Approach to Multiple Testing, JRSS B 57(1) (1995), Theorem 1 and Appendix A",
              "url": "https://www.math.tau.ac.il/~yekutiel/eBayes/bh_1995.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Yoav Benjamini and Daniel Yekutieli, The Control of the False Discovery Rate in Multiple Testing under Dependency, The Annals of Statistics 29(4) (2001), §5, PDF pp. 16–17",
              "url": "https://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf_1/euclid.aos/1013699998"
            },
            {
              "title": "STATS 200: Introduction to Statistical Inference, Lecture 11: Testing Multiple Hypotheses, Stanford University, PDF pp. 32–36",
              "url": "https://web.stanford.edu/class/archive/stats/stats200/stats200.1172/Lecture11.pdf"
            }
